# Pinned sha256 AND origin URL for every third-party archive the harness unpacks.
# Format: <sha256>  <basename>  <url>   — the URL lives beside the hash so a fetch and its
# verification cannot disagree about which file they mean. ONE source of truth: the artifact's
# third-party/SOURCES.md quotes these same values, and `verify_archive.sh` is what enforces them.
#
# A mismatch is a STOP, not a warning: an upstream file that moved or changed silently changes what is
# built, and every number in this repository is attributed to a specific source tree. Unknown archives are
# also refused — failing closed on a file nobody pinned is the point.
#
# Provenance of each pin, because they are not all equally strong:
#
#   memcached, redis, ffmpeg, mysql  verified against the archives the 15-17 September 2026 campaign built
#                                    from, on the machine that built it.
#   memtier                          verified BOTH ways: the archive on this machine and a fresh fetch of
#                                    the GitHub tag are byte-identical. Its local name is 2.1.1.tar.gz —
#                                    GitHub's tag download renamed — and the pinned name here is the
#                                    descriptive one the fetcher uses.
#   sqlite                           THE WEAK ONE. download_and_compile_sqlite.sh deletes the zip after
#                                    unpacking, so only the tree survives from the campaign and this hash
#                                    is from a fresh download of the same versioned URL. Almost certainly
#                                    the same file; we cannot demonstrate the campaign compiled it. Stated
#                                    as a gap rather than presented as equivalent to the other five.
